    <h2 id="win-explainer-heading">How Straight Win picks are built</h2>
    <p>
        Straight Win publishes a home-win or away-win angle in 90 minutes (plus stoppage). It is the
        classic 1X2 side market without the draw. That makes it intuitive — “this side should win” —
        and also unforgiving: a late equaliser or a single clinical counter turns a strong process
        pick into a loss on the scoreboard.
    </p>

    <h3>What you see on the Straight Win card</h3>
    <p>
        Each published row names the backed side (home or away), the fixture context, kickoff, and
        calibrated confidence. Filters help you focus on elite confidence, a specific league, or
        upcoming fixtures only. When a match finishes, the score settles the pick: the backed side
        must win; draws and losses both count as incorrect for Straight Win.
    </p>

    <h3>How a side is chosen</h3>
    <p>
        The ensemble builds 1X2 probabilities from de-vigged bookmaker prices (Shin margin removal
        on the three-way market), Dixon-Coles scoreline probabilities, Elo-adjusted form, and a
        quality-checked third-party feed when present. After blending, the 1X2 triple is renormalised
        to sum to one. A Straight Win pick is published only when the chosen side’s calibrated
        probability clears the side-specific threshold — not merely because the side is favourite
        on the board.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>Home and away wins use their own threshold profiles.</li>
        <li>Favourites below the publish gate stay off the public card.</li>
        <li>Optional ML signals join only after holdout Brier promotion.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Confidence vs “obvious favourite”</h3>
    <p>
        Bookmaker favourites and model confidence often agree — but not always. A short-priced side
        can still miss the publish threshold if calibration says the price is too aggressive, and a
        slightly longer side can clear the gate when statistical form and market prices align. Use
        confidence to rank today’s published wins; use <a href="/results">Results</a> to see how
        recent Straight Win picks actually landed.
    </p>

    <h3>Empty cards and cup chaos</h3>
    <p>
        Rotated squads, two-legged ties, or sparse schedules can leave few sides clearing the gate.
        That is expected. We do not force a Straight Win onto the page to fill space. For multi-leg
        constructions that start from analysed wins, see <a href="/betslips">Betslips</a>. For the
        pipeline detail, see <a href="/methodology">Methodology</a>.
    </p>

    <h3>Responsible use</h3>
    <p>
        Match outcomes are uncertain even when probabilities look high. This site is informational,
        18+, and not a bookmaker. Review <a href="/Privacy#responsible-use">responsible use</a> and
        the <a href="/faq">FAQ</a> before relying on any card.
    </p>
</section>
